//!   shim group is the application alloc/free wrappers ([`fn_801eff00`],
//!   [`fn_801eff50`]), the WUD sync completion ([`fn_801effa0`]), the WBC
//!   personality getter ([`fn_801efef0`]) and the three IOS bulk-message
//!   senders ([`fn_801ef6f0`], [`fn_801ef770`], [`fn_801ef810`]).
//! - `0x801F0B10`-`0x801FF970` - fifteenth pass (wave 102): the rest of the
//!   HID-host receive path and the WUD/security state steps between the earlier
//!   passes. The report group is the two IOS pumps
//!   ([`fn_801f8f70`], [`fn_801f9120`]) with their start/stop pair
//!   ([`fn_801f9490`], [`fn_801f96a0`]), the chunk feeder ([`fn_801f9840`]),
//!   the ACL reassembler ([`fn_801f8bc0`]), the event pump ([`fn_801f9fa0`])
//!   and the HID state machine ([`fn_801ff970`]). The GKI stubs are
//!   [`fn_801f8630`], [`fn_801f8640`], [`fn_801f8650`], [`fn_801f8660`],
//!   [`fn_801f87b0`], [`fn_801f87f0`] and the buffer constant thunk
//!   [`fn_801f3300`]. The security group is [`fn_801fc958`], [`fn_801fcc74`],
//!   [`fn_801fd16c`], [`fn_801fd4bc`] and [`fn_801fd83c`]. The WUD steps are
//!   the connect arming ([`fn_801f36f0`]), the device-table migration
//!   ([`fn_801f1f70`]), the WiiFit save-data ladder ([`fn_801f2650`]) and the
//!   pairing state machine ([`fn_801f0b10`]).
//! - `0x801EB5C0`-`0x801EFE60` - sixteenth pass (wave 103): the IOS USB
//!   transfer layer and the KPAD initialisation/aim path. The USB group is the
//!   heap bring-up ([`fn_801eeb00`]), the completion handler
//!   ([`fn_801eec10`]), `OpenDevice` ([`fn_801eedb0`]), `CloseDeviceAsync`
//!   ([`fn_801eef00`]), the long-bulk/ interrupt senders ([`fn_801ef030`],
//!   [`fn_801ef390`]), the control-transfer builder ([`fn_801ef8b0`]) with its
//!   async entry point ([`fn_801efe60`]) and the `0`-returning stub
//!   ([`fn_801eec00`]). The KPAD group is the record initialiser
//!   ([`fn_801edaf0`]), the sensor-bar matcher ([`fn_801ec2f0`]), the aim step
//!   it feeds ([`fn_801ebd20`]), the extension-report mapper
//!   ([`fn_801ecad0`]) and the `0x801ED3C0` forwarder ([`fn_801ed3b0`]).
//! - `0x801E9730`-`0x801EEA50` - seventeenth pass (wave 106): the two KPAD
//!   random self-test report builders ([`fn_801e9730`], [`fn_801e9e20`]), the
//!   ring-flush read path ([`fn_801ed3c0`]) with its `0x801ED3A0` forwarder
//!   ([`fn_801ed3a0`]) and the two IOS USB error forwarders
//!   ([`fn_801ee9a0`], [`fn_801eea50`]).
//! - `0x801E55C0`-`0x801EE230` - eighteenth pass (wave 110): the DPD and
//!   accelerometer calibration finaliser ([`fn_801e55c0`]) and the per-channel
//!   sampling callback that shares its format handshake with the classic
//!   controller state machine ([`fn_801ee230`]). This is every remaining body
//!   of the `0x801E0000` bucket that the export supports; the two command
//!   record writers stay skipped in `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE110_INPUT.md`.
//! - `0x801E1E20`-`0x801E39C0` - nineteenth pass (wave 112): the two WPAD
//!   command-record writers that close the `0x801E0000` bucket. Both map a mode
//!   word onto fixed 0x30-byte records and push them into the `+0x160` command
//!   ring: [`fn_801e1e20`] queues the extension enable/disable register writes,
//!   [`fn_801e39c0`] the per-extension-kind `0x4B000xx` activation sequence.
//! - `0x801F8820`-`0x801F9C50` - twentieth pass (wave 113): the HCI receive
//!   state machine that reassembles a report from the three-slot block at
//!   `0x803CDDA8` ([`fn_801f8820`]), and the variadic trace forwarder the seven
//!   trace guards branch into ([`fn_801f9c50`]). This closes the
//!   `0x801F0000` bucket; the forwarder takes the caller's register, float and
//!   stack arguments explicitly so its `va_list` frame is complete.
//!
//! **Start here.** [`wpad_init_801df7f0`] publishes the control blocks and
//! arms the alarm; it calls [`wpad_control_init_801df570`], which shows the
//! block layout used by every accessor in this file. [`wpad_start_801dfa50`]
//! is the outer start gate used by game code; the two public setters at the end
//! install sampling/connect callbacks. For the report path start at
//! [`fn_801e0820`] (attach), then [`fn_801e6be0`] (dispatch) and the decoder it
//! selects.
//!
//! **Dependencies.** [`crate::platform`] for big-endian volatile loads/stores
//! and raw calls (`call0`-`call7`); [`crate::runtime::os`] for the `r13`
//! small-data base, the interrupt lock (original pair `0x801B1140`/
//! `0x801B1180`), the 64-bit time base (`0x801B5F80`/`0x801B5FB0`), and the
//! no-argument value-returning call wrapper. `0x800046B4` (`memset`) and
//! `0x80004364` (`memcpy`) are called by address. The Bluetooth link-key
//! helpers `0x801F6160`/`0x801F6350`/`0x80201CD8`, the device-name compare
//! `0x802DF098`, the extension queries `0x801DB8E0`/`0x801DD6D0`/
//! `0x801DD600`/`0x801DCC70` stay in the unpromoted transport lane and are
//! called by address. The report layer additionally calls the report-decoder
//! table `0x80343E90`, the extension probes `0x801DDA50`-`0x801DDA80` and
//! `0x801EFEF0`, the queue helpers `0x801E4EC0`/`0x801E5080`/`0x801E5240`/
//! `0x801EA820`, the XOR decoder `0x801EA520`, the calibration finaliser
//! `0x801E55C0`, and the sin/cos helpers `0x802E84C4`/`0x802E7FBC` by address.
//!
//! **Glossary.** **WPAD** - Wii Remote driver. **KPAD** - higher-level pad
//! layer built on WPAD (see `docs/GLOSSARY.md`). **SDA** - the PowerPC
//! small-data area addressed through `r13`; every `SDA_*` constant is an
//! `r13`-relative global from the Ghidra output. **Control block** - the
//! 0x9C0-byte per-channel state block (`0x8039F360` + channel * 0x9C0).
//! **Link key** - the six-byte Bluetooth pairing key forwarded to
//! `0x80201CD8`.
//!
//! **Promotion note.** These functions were moved here from the address-range
//! staging lane `src/subsystems/r801c0_80200.rs`; their `# Original` evidence
//! blocks are unchanged. The second pass appended the WPAD start/connection and
//! KPAD status/callback helpers from the same `main.dol` band. The third pass
//! adds device assignment, the report-ring producers and the sampling/report
//! readers. The fourth pass (wave 5) adds the ground-truth report pipeline:
//! attach/disconnect, report dispatch and the per-extension decoders, including
//! the calibration records used by the nunchuk and classic controller. The
//! fifth pass (wave 14) adds the queue builders the report pipeline already
//! called by address, the report stability/battery helpers, the Bluetooth
//! link-key slots and the balance-board API stubs. The sixth pass (wave 18)
//! adds the report-queue consumer and the GKI buffer-pool/queue layer it
//! allocates from, including the critical-section stack. The seventh pass
//! (wave 34) adds the BTA SYS timer/registration layer the report-queue
//! consumer's HID-host stack runs on. The eighth pass (wave 50) adds the BTA
//! device-manager message builders and the inquiry/registration glue the
//! WPAD state machine calls by address. The ninth pass (wave 61) adds the
//! HID-host state block, its method-table plumbing, the stack bring-up
//! triad and the `0x1700`..`0x170E` API request builders that sit between the
//! GKI pool and the `0x801FF970` state machine. The tenth pass (wave 74) adds
//! the WUD device-manager state machine (flush callbacks, callback setters,
//! link-up poll helpers and the AFH channel filter), the GKI statistics clear,
//! the two HID-host send pumps and the BTM bring-up with its four
//! remote-request event handlers. The eleventh pass (wave 89) adds the BCM2045
//! firmware patch staging chain above that bring-up, the WUD device-database
//! builders and their two link-list banks, the BTM stored-link-key event
//! handler, the DM disable timer pair, the two inquiry forwarders and the
//! HID-host security-entry/teardown group. The twelfth pass (wave 91) adds the
//! BTA name/discovery search between them: the remote-name request chain, the
//! discovery-slot table, the compressed-link service registry, the PIN-code and
//! link-key callbacks, the WUD shutdown/init handshake and the HID device-event
//! bridge. The thirteenth pass (wave 96) adds the WUD pairing/save state steps
//! and the dynamic GKI pool helpers the earlier passes left between them. The
//! fourteenth pass (wave 97) adds the KPAD channel-block layer, the pointer-aim
//! filters and the IOS/GKI shims below the pairing state machine. The
//! fifteenth pass (wave 102) adds the HID-host receive pumps and event pump,
//! the BTA security/ACL bookkeeping, the four WUD connection/save steps and the
//! remaining GKI task stubs, closing the `0x801F0000` band except for the two
//! bodies listed as skipped in `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE102_INPUT.md`. The sixteenth
//! pass (wave 103) adds the IOS USB transfer layer at the bottom of the
//! `0x801E0000` band and the KPAD record initialiser, sensor-bar matcher, aim
//! step and extension-report mapper that sit between the wave-97 pointer
//! filters. The seventeenth pass (wave 106) adds the two KPAD random self-test
//! report builders, the ring-flush read path behind `0x801ED3A0` and the two
//! IOS USB error forwarders, closing the last ten entries of the `0x801E0000`
//! bucket except for the four bodies listed as skipped in
//! `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE106_INPUT.md`. The eighteenth pass (wave 110) adds the
//! DPD/accelerometer calibration finaliser and the per-channel sampling
//! callback, leaving only the two WPAD command-record writers of that bucket
//! untranslated (see `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE110_INPUT.md`). The nineteenth pass
//! (wave 112) adds those two writers: re-reading the exports showed that the
//! bytes wave 110 treated as a missing payload are never written by the export
//! and lie outside the record length the queue consumer copies, so both
//! sequences are reconstructed from the fields the producers do write (see
//! `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE112_INPUT.md`). The twentieth pass (wave 113) adds the
//! HCI receive dispatch wave 102 skipped: disassembling its sole caller
//! `0x801F8E80` showed that the `0x802DCD40` fold carries the slot index in `r3`
//! and the `0x803CDDA8` block in `r4`, which fixes the pairing the export alone
//! could not prove. It also adds the `0x801F9C50` variadic trace forwarder as a
//! `partial` body, closing the `0x801F0000` bucket (see
//! `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE113_INPUT.md`).
//!
//! **Naming.** [`fn_801dfb40`], [`fn_801dfb90`], [`fn_801dfc40`],
//! [`fn_801dfd10`], [`fn_801e0d30`], [`fn_801e0f00`], [`fn_801dfbd0`],
//! [`fn_801e0ce0`], [`fn_801e10f0`] and [`fn_801e1150`] keep address names
//! because the field, mode or callback-trigger meaning is not proven. Their
//! subsystem evidence is the shared 0x9C0-byte control block and the callers /
//! registration sites cited in each doc.

use crate::boot::entry::SDA2_BASE;
use crate::platform::{self, call7};
use crate::runtime::os::{
    call0_ret, disable_interrupts, get_time, read_time_base, restore_interrupts, SDA_BASE,
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WPAD / KPAD constants
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Pointer table indexed by channel (0..=3); WPAD stores each channel's
/// 0x9C0-byte control block here (`0x8039E330`).
const KPAD_CONTROL_TABLE: usize = 0x8039_E330;

/// Size of one WPAD/KPAD per-channel control block: `0x8039F360`, `0x8039FD20`,
/// `0x803A06E0`, `0x803A10A0` are 0x9C0 bytes apart in the Ghidra output.
const KPAD_CONTROL_STRIDE: usize = 0x9C0;

/// First WPAD per-channel control block (`0x8039F360`).
const WPAD_CONTROL_BASE: usize = 0x8039_F360;

// --- external call targets -------------------------------------------------
//
// The image carries no symbol table, so these names describe the role observed
// in the Ghidra body/callers (all are marked `inferred` in the function docs).

/// `memset` (boot lane), Ghidra `FUN_800046B4`.
const MEMSET: usize = 0x8000_46B4;
/// `memcpy` (boot lane), Ghidra `FUN_80004364`.
const MEMCPY: usize = 0x8000_4364;
/// `0x801EA8F0`: formatted diagnostic printer (`OSReport`-shaped).
const OS_REPORT: usize = 0x801E_A8F0;
/// `0x8015F730`: formatted diagnostic printer used by KPAD setters.
const OS_REPORT_KPAD: usize = 0x8015_F730;
/// `0x801B4B20`: initialises a queue/thread-queue object with one pointer.
const OS_INIT_QUEUE: usize = 0x801B_4B20;
/// `0x801EA7F0`: per-channel WPAD reset hook (one channel argument).
const WPAD_CHANNEL_HOOK: usize = 0x801E_A7F0;
/// `0x801F6160`: returns the six-byte link key for a Bluetooth device ID.
const BT_GET_LINK_KEY: usize = 0x801F_6160;
/// `0x80201CD8`: stores/forwards a six-byte link key.
const BT_STORE_LINK_KEY: usize = 0x8020_1CD8;
/// `0x801AB1D0`: WPAD tunable source (word). Role inside the tunable unknown.
const WPAD_TUNABLE_WORD: usize = 0x801A_B1D0;
/// `0x801AB260`: WPAD tunable source (byte).
const WPAD_TUNABLE_BYTE: usize = 0x801A_B260;
/// `0x801AB1C0`: WPAD callback/argument hook.
const WPAD_ARG_HOOK: usize = 0x801A_B1C0;
/// `0x801AB2E0`: alarm object initialiser (one pointer).
const OS_ALARM_INIT: usize = 0x801A_B2E0;
/// `0x801AB5B0`: periodic-alarm setter (seven arguments).
const OS_ALARM_SET_PERIODIC: usize = 0x801A_B5B0;
/// `0x801DD530`: WPAD config query, clamped to 1..=5 by init.
const WPAD_CONFIG_1_TO_5: usize = 0x801D_D530;
/// `0x801DD610`: WPAD config query reduced to a single boolean by init.
const WPAD_CONFIG_BOOL_A: usize = 0x801D_D610;
/// `0x801DD5A0`: WPAD config query reduced to a single boolean by init.
const WPAD_CONFIG_BOOL_B: usize = 0x801D_D5A0;
/// `0x801DD670`: WPAD config query, clamped to 0..=0x7F by init.
const WPAD_CONFIG_0_TO_7F: usize = 0x801D_D670;
/// `0x801DF550`: periodic sampling-alarm handler installed by WPAD init.
const WPAD_ALARM_HANDLER: u32 = 0x801D_F550;

// --- r13-relative WPAD/KPAD globals ---------------------------------------

/// `r13-0x4BD0`: one init byte per channel, cleared by `0x801DF570`.
const SDA_WPAD_4BD0: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BD0;
/// `r13-0x4C08`: one byte per channel, cleared by `0x801DF570`/`0x801DF7F0`.
const SDA_WPAD_4C08: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4C08;
/// `r13-0x4C04`: one byte per channel, cleared by `0x801DF570`/`0x801DF7F0`.
const SDA_WPAD_4C04: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4C04;
/// `r13-0x4BC2`: byte initialised to 5 by WPAD init; later written by
/// [`fn_801e0f00`] (a game caller passes 10).
const SDA_WPAD_4BC2: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BC2;
/// `r13-0x4BC0`: word initialised from `0x801AB1D0`; role unknown.
const SDA_WPAD_4BC0: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BC0;
/// `r13-0x4BBC`: byte initialised from `0x801AB260`; role unknown.
const SDA_WPAD_4BBC: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BBC;
/// `r13-0x4BC3`: byte initialised from `0x801DD530`, clamped to 1..=5.
const SDA_WPAD_4BC3: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BC3;
/// `r13-0x4BC4`: byte initialised to `CLZ(x-1) >> 5` from `0x801DD610`.
const SDA_WPAD_4BC4: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BC4;
/// `r13-0x4BC8`: word initialised to `CLZ(x-1) >> 5` from `0x801DD5A0`.
const SDA_WPAD_4BC8: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BC8;
/// `r13-0x4BC9`: byte initialised from `0x801DD670`, clamped to 0..=0x7F.
const SDA_WPAD_4BC9: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BC9;
/// `r13-0x4C0E`: 16-bit field cleared at the end of WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_4C0E: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4C0E;
/// `r13-0x4C0C`: byte field cleared at the end of WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_4C0C: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4C0C;
/// `r13-0x4C0A`: 16-bit field cleared at the end of WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_4C0A: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4C0A;
/// `r13-0x4BCB`: byte flag cleared at the end of WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_4BCB: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BCB;
/// `r13-0x4BCA`: byte flag set to 1 at the end of WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_4BCA: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BCA;
/// `r13-0x4BCC`: byte set to `0xFF` at the end of WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_4BCC: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BCC;
/// `r13-0x4BD8`: non-zero once KPAD callbacks are live; the setters warn when
/// it is set and a callback is overwritten.
const SDA_KPAD_INITIALISED: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4BD8;
/// `r13-0x5E90`: argument passed to `0x801AB1C0` during WPAD init.
const SDA_WPAD_5E90: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x5E90;

/// `0xCD0000C0`: MMIO register whose bit `0x100` is set under the interrupt
/// lock at the start of WPAD init.
// unknown: exact Hollywood block (EXI-adjacent) is not established by the
// Ghidra output; only the read-modify-write is observed.
const MMIO_CD0000C0: usize = 0xCD00_00C0;

/// `0x800000F8`: bus/CPU clock word used to derive the WPAD alarm period.
const LOW_MEM_BUS_CLOCK: usize = 0x8000_00F8;

/// `"WPADInit()..."` diagnostic at `0x80343BD0`.
const MSG_WPAD_INIT: u32 = 0x8034_3BD0;
/// `"WARNING! Overwritten the callback..."` at `0x80343CD8`.
const MSG_KPAD_OVERWRITE: u32 = 0x8034_3CD8;
/// `"Please call KPADSetSamplingCallback..."` at `0x80343D0C`.
const MSG_KPAD_SAMPLING_HINT: u32 = 0x8034_3D0C;
/// `"Please call KPADSetConnectCallback..."` at `0x80343D60`.
const MSG_KPAD_CONNECT_HINT: u32 = 0x8034_3D60;

/// Reads the per-channel KPAD control-block pointer from
/// [`KPAD_CONTROL_TABLE`].
///
/// # Original
/// - Inlined accessor: every WPAD/KPAD function here reads
///   `0x8039E330 + channel * 4` directly; no standalone function was emitted
///   for it in the Ghidra export.
///
/// # Safety
/// `channel` must be `0..=3` and WPAD/KPAD must have been initialised.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the table is written by `FUN_801df7f0` and read with this stride.
unsafe fn kpad_control(channel: u32) -> usize {
    platform::read_u32(KPAD_CONTROL_TABLE + channel as usize * 4) as usize
}

/// Resets one WPAD channel's 0x9C0-byte control block to its power-on defaults.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_801df570` at `0x801DF570` (628 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/801df570_FUN_801df570.c`
/// - Called by WPAD init (`0x801DF7F0`) and the data handler (`0x801E0820`).
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `channel` - 0..=3. Recovered from the prologue artifact `0x802DCD70`
///   (r3 is preserved); Ghidra shows the function as `void`.
///
/// # Returns
/// `()`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Clears fields, zeroes sub-buffers with `memset`, copies a 0x60-byte block,
/// installs two internal ring buffers (`+0x16C`/`+0x5F8`), stores two 64-bit
/// time snapshots (`0x801B5FB0`) at `+0x8F0`/`+0x908`, and clears the two
/// per-channel SDA bytes. The two ring-buffer clears run under the interrupt
/// lock.
///
/// # Safety
/// The channel table entry must already point at a writable 0x9C0-byte block
/// (`WPADInit` establishes it before calling this).
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - every store is transcribed; the block's semantic field names are
/// unknown and therefore not invented.
pub unsafe fn wpad_control_init_801df570(channel: u32) {
    let ctl = kpad_control(channel);

    // observed: literal power-on values copied from the C. The control block
    // has no recovered field names; `unknown:` what each value enables, so
    // they are kept verbatim rather than guessed.
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x850, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8D4, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x89C, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8A0, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8A8, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8AC, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8B0, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8B4, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8B8, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8BC, 0xFFFF_FFFF);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8C1, 0xFD);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8C2, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x911, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x912, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8C0, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8CA, 0x0C);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8CB, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8CC, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x8F8, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x8FE, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x8FA, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x900, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x8FC, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x902, 0);

    let t0 = get_time();
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8F0, (t0 >> 32) as u32);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8F4, t0 as u32);
    let t1 = get_time();
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x908, (t1 >> 32) as u32);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x90C, t1 as u32);

    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x910, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8C9, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8C4, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x974, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x978, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x980, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x97C, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8C3, 0xFF);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8D8, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8DC, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8E0, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8CD, 1);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8CE, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8CF, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x982, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x983, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x984, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x986, 1);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x98C, 0);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x988, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x98E, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x98F, 4);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x990, 0xFD);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x991, 0);
    platform::write_u16(ctl + 0x992, 0);

    // observed: sub-buffer clears, sizes exactly as listed in the C.
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x838) as u32, 0, 0x18);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x934) as u32, 0, 0x40);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x854) as u32, 0, 0x2E);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x882) as u32, 0, 0x1A);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x914) as u32, 0, 0x10);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x924) as u32, 0, 8);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0x92C) as u32, 0, 8);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, ctl as u32, 0, 0x38);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x8C8, 0);
    platform::call3(MEMSET, (ctl + 0xA0) as u32, 0, 0xC0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0xC9, 0xFF);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x129, 0xFF);
    platform::call3(MEMCPY, (ctl + 0x40) as u32, (ctl + 0xA0) as u32, 0x60);

    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x38, 0xFFFF_FFFF);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x3C, 0xFFFF_FFFF);

    // observed: two ring buffers described by (base, capacity) pairs; each
    // slot is 0x30 bytes, so 0x18 slots and 0x0C slots respectively.
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x164, (ctl + 0x16C) as u32);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x168, 0x18);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x5F0, (ctl + 0x5F8) as u32);
    platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x5F4, 0x0C);

    let token = disable_interrupts();
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x160, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x161, 0);
    platform::call3(
        MEMSET,
        platform::read_u32(ctl + 0x164),
        0,
        platform::read_u32(ctl + 0x168).wrapping_mul(0x30),
    );
    restore_interrupts(token);

    let token = disable_interrupts();
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x5EC, 0);
    platform::write_u8(ctl + 0x5ED, 0);
    platform::call3(
        MEMSET,
        platform::read_u32(ctl + 0x5F0),
        0,
        platform::read_u32(ctl + 0x5F4).wrapping_mul(0x30),
    );
    restore_interrupts(token);

    platform::call1(WPAD_CHANNEL_HOOK, channel);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4C08 + channel as usize, 0);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4C04 + channel as usize, 0);
}

/// Initialises the WPAD driver: power-on MMIO bit, per-channel control blocks,
/// driver tunables and the periodic sampling alarm.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_801df7f0` at `0x801DF7F0` (604 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/801df7f0_FUN_801df7f0.c`
/// - String: `"WPADInit()..."` at `0x80343BD0`.
///
/// # Arguments
/// None.
///
/// # Returns
/// `()`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// * Sets bit `0x100` of MMIO `0xCD0000C0` under the interrupt lock.
/// * Writes 16 bytes of `0xFF` at `0x8039F340`.
/// * Publishes four control-block pointers in [`KPAD_CONTROL_TABLE`], runs
///   [`wpad_control_init_801df570`] for each, then initialises
///   `+0x8E4` with `0x801B4B20`.
/// * Fills `r13` tunables from `0x801AB1D0`, `0x801AB260`, `0x801DD530`,
///   `0x801DD610`, `0x801DD5A0`, `0x801DD670`; invokes four optional callbacks
///   at `r13-0x4BF8`, `-0x4BF4`, `-0x4BF0`, `-0x4BEC`.
/// * Arms a periodic alarm with `0x801AB5B0`, using `bus_clock / 4000` and
///   handler `0x801DF550`.
///
/// # Safety
/// Must run once, after the OS/heap are up. The optional function pointers in
/// the SDA must be null or valid.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the `WPADInit()` string plus the four-channel control-block setup
/// identify the role; the body is a direct transcription.
pub unsafe fn wpad_init_801df7f0() {
    let token = disable_interrupts();
    // observed: read-modify-write of a Hollywood MMIO bit while interrupts are
    // off; the bit is cleared only by hardware reset.
    let mmio = platform::read_u32(MMIO_CD0000C0) | 0x100;
    platform::write_u32(MMIO_CD0000C0, mmio);
    restore_interrupts(token);

    // observed: 16 bytes of 0xFF at 0x8039F340..0x8039F34F.
    for i in 0..16 {
        platform::write_u8(0x8039_F340 + i, 0xFF);
    }

    platform::call1(OS_REPORT, MSG_WPAD_INIT);

    let mut channel = 0u32;
    while channel < 4 {
        let ctl = WPAD_CONTROL_BASE + channel as usize * KPAD_CONTROL_STRIDE;
        platform::write_u32(KPAD_CONTROL_TABLE + channel as usize * 4, ctl as u32);
        platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BD0 + channel as usize, 0);
        platform::write_u32(ctl + 0x8A4, 0); // connect callback slot
        wpad_control_init_801df570(channel);
        platform::call1(OS_INIT_QUEUE, (ctl + 0x8E4) as u32);
        platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4C08 + channel as usize, 0);
        platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4C04 + channel as usize, 0);
        channel += 1;
    }

    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BC2, 5);
    platform::write_u32(SDA_WPAD_4BC0, call0_ret(WPAD_TUNABLE_WORD));
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BBC, call0_ret(WPAD_TUNABLE_BYTE) as u8);

    // observed: clamp to 1..=5, zero becomes 1.
    let n = call0_ret(WPAD_CONFIG_1_TO_5) as u8;
    let n = if n == 0 {
        1
    } else if n > 5 {
        5
    } else {
        n
    };
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BC3, n);

    // observed: CLZ(x-1) >> 5 is a compact "x == 1" test.
    let v = (call0_ret(WPAD_CONFIG_BOOL_A) & 0xFF).wrapping_sub(1);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BC4, (v.leading_zeros() >> 5) as u8);
    let v = (call0_ret(WPAD_CONFIG_BOOL_B) & 0xFF).wrapping_sub(1);
    platform::write_u32(SDA_WPAD_4BC8, v.leading_zeros() >> 5);

    // observed: clamp to 0..=0x7F.
    let n = call0_ret(WPAD_CONFIG_0_TO_7F) as u8;
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BC9, if n > 0x7E { 0x7F } else { n });

    platform::write_u16(SDA_WPAD_4C0E, 0);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4C0C, 0);
    platform::write_u16(SDA_WPAD_4C0A, 0);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BCB, 0);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BCA, 1);
    platform::write_u8(SDA_WPAD_4BCC, 0xFF);
    platform::write_u32(SDA_KPAD_INITIALISED, 0);

    platform::call1(WPAD_ARG_HOOK, platform::read_u32(SDA_WPAD_5E90));

    for off in [0x4BF8usize, 0x4BF4, 0x4BF0, 0x4BEC] {
        let callback = platform::read_u32(SDA_BASE - off);
        if callback != 0 {
            platform::call0(callback as usize);
        }
    }

    platform::call1(OS_ALARM_INIT, 0x8039_E300);
    let time = read_time_base();
    let period = platform::read_u32(LOW_MEM_BUS_CLOCK) / 4000;
    call7(
        OS_ALARM_SET_PERIODIC,
        0x8039_E300,
        time as u32,
        (time >> 32) as u32,
        time as u32,
        0,
        period,
        WPAD_ALARM_HANDLER,
    );
}

/// Reads one per-channel status byte at control-block offset `+0x983` under the
/// interrupt lock.
///
/// # Original
